===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|forme||shape, figure, manner, bench, frame, seat, condition, agreement, etc.}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|forme}}, from {{der|en|la|fōrma||shape, figure, image, outline, plan, mold, frame, case, etc., manner, sort, kind, etc.}}.

In sense "division grouping school students" (now dated), derived from [[public school]] nomenclature later adopted by state schools. It is sometimes said to be from the sense of "bench", where students of certain ages would sit together,<ref>{{cite-book|author=Hensleigh Wedgwood|authorlink=Hensleigh Wedgwood|url=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofengl00wedgrich/page/274/mode/2up|year=1871|title=A Dictionary of English Etymology|page=274|section=Sense 2.|text=The name of {{m|la|forma}} was also given to the seat of the choristers in a cathedral and the desk in front of them. {{m|en|formula|Formula}}, a stool to kneel on.—Duc. There can be no doubt that this is essentially the same application with the name of the classes at our public schools, first ''form'', {{m-self|en|sixth form}}, &c., but whether the class is called ''form'' from sitting on the same bench, or whether the bench is so designated from being occupied by a single class, may be a question. It seems certain that {{m|la|forma}} was used for class or order in the lower Latin. 'Supernumerarii sacri ministerii primæ vel secundæ {{m|la|formae|formæ}},' of the first or second order.—Cod. Theodos. de Castrensianis in Duc.}}</ref> though this is disputed,<ref>{{R:OED Online|form||6235838709|subentry=sense I.6.b.|passage=The word is usually explained as meaning originally ‘a number of scholars sitting on the same form’ (sense  II.17); but there appears to be no ground for this.}}</ref> or alternatively from the sense of "established method of expression or practice".<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|form||From 1550s as "a class or rank at school" (from sense "a fixed course of study," late 14c.)}}</ref>

